re going to have to  do conventional chromatography  and you could assume by starting that  you're going to do a three column  process  and so let's say that you for the first  column that you're going to look at for  the first column in the process  you're going to look at an anion  exchange column now for that single  column you're going to have to  optimize the column type whether it's q  or d-e-a-e which vendor  you're going to have to look at the ph  and the conductivity you're going to  have to look at residence time different  volumes wash volumes  and you're going to have to look at  regeneration protocols and each one of  these parameters is going to require a  combinatorial set of experiments  there's very clever ways to do it with  design of experiments you can have  robots do a whole bunch of experiments  but at the end of the day you're you're  going to end up doing this for every  single column in this three column  process  and each protein is going to require a  certain column  in a certain order where each column is  optimized for that product  and at the end of all of this it can  take six months or longer to develop a  commercial scale process  if you don't have any kind of a common  feature so you have to start from  scratch with each new protein  and this leads to some real real  problems with developing these drugs it  slows it down  it creates risk which the industry hates  and so the question is can we come up  with  a platform for everything else a single  affinity capture that would be able to  purify all these proteins  and of course those of you who work in  the area of protein expression  especially at high throughput research  and laboratory scale  you say well of course there is we'll  just put on an affinity tag  and so the affinity tags you're cloning  the dna of the target protein into  whatever cell you want to make that  protein in  but then you add a little piece of dna  to it that creates a tag so that when  that protein gets ex