good morning everyone  and um thanks for coming to my talk i'm  professor david wood at ohio state  university  and for about the last 20 years i've  been working on a technology involving  self-cleaving affinity tags  i'm in a position now where we've  co-founded a company and are hoping to  commercialize this technology in the  next couple or three months  and i wanted to tell you a little bit  about where it came from what it can do  and where i think it's going  and so what i wanted to do was just to  get us all on the same page  do a very very quick introduction into  biopharmaceuticals because this is  really where we see this tag making a  big difference  and so as you know biopharmaceuticals  are very large drugs they're  polypeptides  much larger than conventional synthetic  molecules  and as a result they need to be  expressed in some kind of a living host  cell  bacterial expression e coli and  mammalian expression of heck and cho  are probably the two most common ones  but there's yeast and insect  systems as well so as a consequence of  these  complex molecules being made in complex  cells there's quite a lot of impurities  that have to be removed  before these drugs can be injected into  a human patient  and really where our technology has its  impact is in this purification this  downstream processing  of the of the purification of the  proteins and of course the main thing  that we worry about  is host cell proteins and getting rid of  those but  really getting rid of all of these and  getting the drug in a way that it can be  injected into a human  and so a consequence of the  biopharmaceutical industry  the the large profit margins for these  drugs  has led to a couple of important points  that kind of dominate the thinking of  the industry  and one is that the speed to market is  absolutely critical  the way i explain it to my students is  if you have a drug that's going to make  a billion dollars a year  that translates to roughly three million  dollars a day  so for every week that that that drug is  delayed  um it's costing basically the salaries  of everybody who's ever worked on that  drug  and so the speed to market is really  critical  and part of what comes out of that is  that the industries become very  conservative so if there's any risk  associated with the drug  they really don't want to take a lot of  risks and they like to keep their own  platforms there is some pressure to  innovate and as things change that  pressure is becoming  greater but right now the industry would  like to stick to the technologies that  they know and that they know that they  can get past the fda  and those two things coming together  create the third point which is that a  lot of these drugs are made using  established platforms especially for  monoclonal antibodies  so if you have a different drugs they're  all basically made the same ways  and some of the things that are changing  now is that the cost of goods are  starting to become important especially  as biosimilars are being released  and there's a lot of new modalities on  the horizon with things like gene  therapies  and exosome delivery vehicles and new  types of  antibodies and antibody fragments but if  we go back and kind of look at this sort  of big established platform  um what we start with is a bioreactor  that has our cells whether they're e  coli or mammalian  uh this system that i'm showing you is a  perfusion system but one way or another  that  that cell brought gets clarified  and put onto a series of chromatography  columns the first one is usually  referred to as the primary capture  and so these chromatography columns when  i'm explaining this to investors and  and other people who don't know much  about bioprocessing they're like brita  water filters where you  put a liquid through the liquid has a  whole bunch of stuff in it like a  vegetable soup and if you're lucky  your brita water filter will grab on to  just what you want  which would