well the problem solved then we're just  going to put tags on everything and and  do that for clinical research  but here's the problem uh the first is  that when you go to scale this up the  protease enzymes are actually quite  expensive  they have to be produced at gmp quality  in order to be introduced  to your gmp quality process you still  have to do the additional processing  but what's even more of a headache  especially in high throughput research  is that sometimes these proteases  randomly chop up your product protein  and then every other times you add the  protease and it just doesn't cleave off  the tag  and you don't know why and people talk  about stereo kindreds and other things  but it becomes something that even  though it works great and people do it  at laboratory scale  uh proteolytic cleavage especially with  an endopeptide like this  is never used in bioprocessing ever you  will not find a drug where there's a  protease removal of a tag  i heard a rumor once that there's one  where they use an exopeptidase to get  rid of a his tag but i was never able to  identify  which protein that was so even though  some of you may know something that i  don't  i think all of us would agree that this  isn't extraordinarily uncommon in  manufacturing  so there's your problem you need to use  an affinity tag at research scale where  you don't have six months to look at  each new target  but at manufacturing scale you don't you  can't have an affinity tag so what often  happens in companies is they say okay  i'm just gonna leave the tag on and i'm  gonna do all of my pre-clinical studies  with the tags so i'm going to do my  binding studies  my assay my talk studies but deep down i  know  that if i go into clinical trials with  that tag protein  i'm going to get blocked the fda is  going to have a fit and they're going to  say you got to get rid of that tag  before you start having patients with  immune responses  and the upshot of all of this is that if  you  are going from discovery to clinical  manufacturing  in discovery you're going to have  tag-based systems you don't really have  a choice because you have too many  proteins coming down the pipeline  and not enough time to figure out a  formal process for purifying them  on the other hand when you go into  clinical manufacturing you're going to  have to  come up with a complicated new process  that's going to take months to develop  and at some point between discovery and  manufacturing you're going to have to  make that transition  and so this slide was made by my student  joe terrace who's graduating soon  and has done quite a lot of the work  that i'm going to be showing you today  um but i have a little picture of him  because i blatantly stole this slide  from him and i figured i should  uh keep his photo on it at least but  i'll talk about him at the very end  so that really comes into where my talk  is so the idea is can we  engineer a new universal purification  platform but that will create a tagless  target  and is therefore useful at research  skills for high throughput applications  where you need to look at a lot of  different proteins in a hurry  but then can go seamlessly into  manufacturing and deliver a protein that  the fda would consider safe  and so the story begins with the  discovery of this kind of protein  elements called an  intene that's a self-splicing protein so  those of you who do a lot of molecular  biology remember self-splicing introns  and in team splices at the protein level  and forgive me if you've already heard a  lot about in teens  when i was starting my phd in teens had  just been discovered  i started my phd in 1993 and for the  first 10 or 15 years of my career  i spent a lot of time explaining to  audiences even in biology conferences  what in teams were  and somewhere along the way it seems  that they've gotten into textbooks and  now even the undergrads in my classes  know what they are and they sort of roll  their eyes when i explain them again but  some  habits are hard to break but in any case  the intent can self-splice and what's  cool about the intent is you can move it  to a different context and it will still  function it's completely self-contained  at least many in teams and so pretty  quickly  the splicing mechanism was figured out  and i'm not going to go into detail on  that  but an upshot of that understanding of  the splicing mechanism was the  generation of intunes that rather than  splicing the two x deans together  could either cleave the ecstine from one  terminus or cleave the extine from  another terminus  and so the n-terminal cleaving is  generally induced by thiol addition  and it's a chemical cleavage of a  thioester that forms during the first  step of the splicing and then the  splicing is arrested  so the thiol effectively cleaves the  thioester bond  the c terminal cleaving on the other  hand is induced by a temperature ph  change so it's spontaneous but is  sensitive  at least a little bit to the  environmental conditions  and so as a phd student i'd been working  at amgen for a couple years making  neupogen  and i knew that adding 50 millimolar  thial to anything is not going to be  scalable  so i decided to focus all of my efforts  on c terminal cleaving and we came up  with the big idea  and the big idea was that we'd still  have our affinity tag our binding domain  but  now we'd add an intent between the  affinity tag and the product protein  we could express that as a tripartite  fusion protein  and then go ahead bind wash but instead  of eluding and then using a protease to  cleave off the tag what we would do is  we would simply trigger  the tag to self-cleave by the ending  mechanism  and release the product now tagless from  the bottom of the column  and this was very cool and in my phd  work we  started with our mycobacterium  tuberculosis in teen  this is not a picture of it but it's a  picture of one that looks just like it  we deleted the homing endonuclease  domain to create a minion team to make  the system more efficient  we went ahead and did some directed  evolution using a genetic selection  based on thimodillate synthase  and that genetic selection allowed us to  find a cleavage mutation  and this figure is from our first my  first first author paper that ended up  in nature biotechnology which was pretty  nice  and what the cleavage mutation does is  it allows  the ph sensitivity of the cleaving rate  to become  greatly accentuated so what you see is  the rate constant in the y-axis  the ph and the x-axis and as you go from  ph 8 to about six  one of these in teens suddenly cleaves  much much faster  and that inseam became known as delta  icm  and so delta icm it turns out is also  sensitive to temperature and ph and so  it gives you a lot of levers to pull if  you're going to try to control this  the cleaving activity and so the big  idea was that you could  express the protein at relatively low  temperature in e coli where the  intracellular ph is about seven and a  half  you get an uncleave precursor protein  which then you could stabilize by lysing  the cells at ph 8.5  do your binding and washing at ph 8.5  and then shift the ph  induce cleaving and get your clarified  your purified product out the bottom of  the column  in a single step where really all you've  had to do is change the ph  which is quite trivial compared to  adding a protease  and so pretty quickly we went ahead and  published this in teen and here's a  protein gel  in lames one and two it's the whole cell  lysate in the clarified lysate  of a maltose with an in ten and the  target protein in this case is human  acidic fibroblast growth factor  uh we induce the cleaving reaction in  this case by putting a ph front slowly  through the column and allowing the  protein to cleave and collect as a peak  and then at the end of the process we  just add maltose buffer and we can  regenerate the tag  strip off the tag regenerate the resin  and what you see is that the precursor  protein is almost completely depleted  and all you see really left on the  column is cleave tag  but this this gel shows sort of really  high purity that you can get from a  single  uh in ten resin and we had under loaded  the column i will admit  because maltose tags tends to leech off  a lot  but here's another example where we  purified a subunit of rna polymerase and  again  in this in this gel we have the time  course so in about two and a half hours  we were able to collect a very highly  purified product peak  again with maltospining protein as the  tag and our delta cm antene  in this case we raised the column  temperature to 30 degrees to accelerate  the cleaving reaction but in two and a  half hours we could collect a fairly  nice  concentrated and a highly purified peak  from a single column  so we were very excited we published a  bunch of papers  and of course we filed a patent and the  patent issued several years ago  i think 2005. and  um and we you know  we were off to the races we were all  going to become millionaires  we found companies that were excited  about this and it was going to be great  now what we didn't know or we sort of  knew is that new england biolabs was  also developing their own  uh intent-based technology that in this  case was induced by file edition  and it became known as the impact system  um  and they were able to get this system to  work  and they also publish papers and i have  to admit that a lot of the early  really breakthrough work uh on the ante  mechanism was done in new england bio  labs  along with a few academic labs so they  really added a lot to the field  but unfortunately they also decided to  file some patents  and if you've heard of the impact system  and you've never heard of delta icm  the reason is because they got to the  patent office before us  and so if you've used any intent  technology you probably  remember this figure from the brochure  and you