ing gel  as well but i would argue this is really  kind of a nice purification for a single  column  i would argue that's more pure than even  what you would get from a his tag  but this is actually bona fide gcsf  identical to the amgen product  and so on on the right side you see the  sec that shows that it's monomer with  very little aggregate and then we did an  activity assay that showed  that the uh that the the material was  actually more active than the standard  that we bought  but going back to the 20 hours so any of  you who work in processing are like whoa  whoa we're not going to want to wait 20  hours especially room temperature  so what are you going to do about that  and that led to the other problem which  which we had  20 years ago variable unpredictable  cleaving rates  and so we we tried a whole bunch of  different proteins with this system  and what you see is that you know some  of them cleave faster or slower than  others so streptokinase cleaves pretty  well  but green fluorescent protein in panel b  in the same amount of time is maybe  60 or 50 percent cleaved and so that led  to  really the key breakthrough in all of  this and this was  a predictive model for anti-cleaving so  the question was  where is that variability coming from  and is there a way for us to  solve it and that's where the next sort  of  key experiment came along so in this  experiment what we did is we took  gcsf which we knew cleaved slow it took  20 hours to cleave  overnight now it turns out the first  five amino acids on gcsf  are mtplg so what we did is we took  those five amino acids  and we put them on the front end of  green fluorescent protein  and what you see in the graph is that  the green fluorescent protein then  behaves almost identically to gcsf  in terms of cleaving rate in a second  experiment we took streptokinase  which was known to cleave quite quickly  and very well behaved  it starts with mi agp  we put mi agp on the front of green  fluorescent protein  and sure enough the mi agp  then behaves exactly as streptokinase  and so the implication was  that the first five amino acids on the  front end of the target protein have  this profound influence on how the  target protein behaves  and in fact the target protein may not  matter at all and all these theories  that had been coming along for 20 years  about steric hindrance and the active  site and all kinds of other things  may actually not be relevant so what we  did is we  we took the native extine residues which  are cfn that's what normally follows  this intent in nature  and we put them on the front end of  enhanced green fluorescent protein  and then what we did is we varied the  first amino acid the cysteine to all of  the other 20 native amino acids  and what we found was that sure enough  that first amino acid has a huge  influence on the cleavage rate  so what you see in the y-axis is the  cleaving half-life in hours  where aromatic residues tyrosine  phenylalanine cleave very very slowly  whereas charged residues and small  residues alanine glycine asparagine  those tend to cleave much more slowly  and up to that point  we've been so yeah slower and faster up  to that point  we've been using methionine on the front  of pretty much all of our targets  because  most people when they think of  expressing a protein especially in e  coli  think of it as being methionine  um but it turns out that mt  which is the first two amino acids of  gcsf  they cleave slowly whereas mi  which are the first three amino acids of  streptokinase that cleaves much faster  and those cleavage behaviors the slower  for mt  the faster for mi is consistent with  what we  saw in the plus one amino acids so the  suggestion was  it might be that the first two or three  amino acids matter but they might matter  in a way that's consistent and sort of  additive to how  the amino acid behaves in the first  position so to test this hypothesis  what we did is we took our uh  the first the cfm we put it on the front  end of the  enhanced green fluorescent protein but  then we varied the first and second  position  relative to different families of amino  acids so if we did all 20 by 20 it would  be 400 clones  and we didn't have the resources for  that so we did a 6x6 and did 36 clones  and we came up with these data so each  one of these panels has the same first  amino acid and each line within the  panel  has the same second amino acid and i  often refer to this slide as sort of the  rosetta stone of why  intent research has been such a pain for  so long  what you see is that if you put an  aromatic of phenylalanine on the front  end pretty much everything behaves  really well  on the other hand if you put a proline  on the front end it abolishes cleaving  and you're done  but with methionine which was where most  of our work had been uh done  up to that point what you see is  the um the the cleaving rates are very  variable  and have a profound impact of the second  amino acid  and probably a little bit of the third  and so this explains why we were getting  all this highly variable cleavage rate  all of those years  and in fact if you look at the sort of  middle cleaving ones  a e and k that are sort of the middle  line cleaving a little bit slowly  what you see is e k and a a e and k  all cleave on the slower end of the  spectrum whereas if you look at the ones  at the bottom mmn and mfn that cleave  very very quickly and are basically done  at five hours  that m and that f are on the faster end  of the spectrum  and so the idea was that we could  predict  how fast a protein was going to cleave  based on its first two or three amino  acids  and more importantly if we knew what a  protein was going to cleave slowly we'd  be able to fix it we had ways of doing  it  and so with gcsf again it takes 20 hours  to cleave overnight  the wild type starts with mtp but now i  can look at the data that i have and say  hey you either want to change the first  or second amino acid to phenylalanine  and sure enough if you change the first  amino alanine to  from methionine to phenylalanine you  have ftp  and now you have a really well behaved  protein that cleaves quite quickly  and so this was actually the  breakthrough that made me think that  that we were ready to commercialize this  technology  and before you complain that if you  modify the front of the intent it's not  or the  target protein it's not native human  protein  this is a table that we threw together  pretty quickly where we have  released approved drugs and their trade  names  the actual n-terminus of the human  native protein  and the n-terminus of the drug substance  and the underlying amino acids are the  ones that have been  modified in order to make the drug  easier to manufacture  or expressible in e coli in the case of  gcsf  and along with that we have the proposed  changes that would allow these drugs to  change  to to um work with our intune and so the  point is that the pharmaceutical  industry has been changing the front end  of proteins for  since the beginning i mean gcsf goes  clear back to the beginning  in order to make them easier to um to  manufacture  and as a result we can use this in team  to make therapeutic proteins that are  perfectly consistent with drugs that  have already been approved many many  drugs that have already been approved  and so if we go back to this new  affinity capture resin  we have the ph sensitivity check with  our sensitivity enhancing domain  we have rapid cleaving with the  different mutations that we've  rationally introduced and what's really  nice is we now have reliable performance  we understand  why some in tunes cleave slower why some  in tunes cleave faster  and we know how to fix it and we know  how to predict it  and then finally i won't go into the  detail on all of these but we're looking  at  high binding capacities that we're  optimizing now  i think cheap is probably an optimistic  word  um it's not going to be cheap but it  will be in the same ballpark as a  protein a resin  or you know a fairly nice affinity resin  but it's also highly reusable we've  reused we've regenerated these resins  about 50 times now  without any loss of activity and the  regeneration protocol is just a mild  acid wash  it's nothing exotic and so  we've gone ahead and patent these we  have several issued patents now we have  some others pending  and um and we went ahead and created  this product and so the glossy brochure  the icap tag resin the customer makes  their their tag protein in their system  bind washy loot and regenerate the resin  and so what i wanted to do to kind of  wrap up was show you a few of the case  studies that we've done  this was a slide that came from the very  first run on the very first column that  we ever poured  and what we're doing is we're purifying  super folder gfp out of e coli  and what you see is the column itself in  the lower right  and the little green fluorescent protein  and the test tubes that came out  and a gel showing sort of the purity  that we got and again  first try with the first column that we  ever poured we got a nice yield nice  purity  and and we were very excited about this  result so we started trying some other  things one is we tried to  do streptokinase what you see is that we  have some protease problems so we get a  nice purity but there's definitely some  degradation bounds down below it  and uh it turns out that we looked into  this  and if you add protease inhibitors your  favorite  cocktail of protease inhibitors you can  actually clean up the protease problems  but what's cool is because the intent  actually  has a different mechanism than any of  the serine proteases that you're trying  to inhibit  the intent will still cleave perfectly  happily in the presence of protease  inhibitors  so moving on here's another example of  gcsf that we did from a later  optimization  again there's some bands that we need to  get rid of um  but my argument is if you put something  that pure into a polishing step you'd be  able to clean it up pretty quickly  um but then the exciting part comes so  in this case we're  purifying a single chain antibody from  xb293  so this is mammalian expression we're  able to get really nice secretion  into the cell culture supernatant and  again  uh in the aleutian bands i would argue  that you would be hard-pressed to find a  significant impurity  and the top is a is a silver stain gel  on the bottom is a western blood  and so we were able to show that we can  get good secretion we're using the serum  albumin leader sequence  but it seems to work quite well we can  get good expression of these proteins  um and then we went ahead and did mature  interferon from xb293 so again you don't  need the leader sequence because the  ending cleaves  off but the cool thing about this case  study even though we do have some  um we do have some degradation we got  good secretion  good expression good cleaving good  purity but what i like to point out is  that in this case  we were able actually to actually make  mature interferon with the true  native human and terminus um so that was  it's even more human than the  therapeutic protein based on interferon  that you can buy  and so again gcsf made in mammalian  cells another really nice purification  and then for the first time in this talk  we've actually managed to make ippo so  epigene  poetin expressed again in xv293  and again i would make the argument it  expresses well it goes into the  supernatant  it's completely bound in the flow  through and then after the cleavage  reaction  i would argue that that's a really  nicely purified glycoprotein  and i will have to admit that when we  strip the column that's what the cip is  clean in place  we see some aggregated epo a lot  frankly and we're still trying to figure  out how to optimize the buffer is to  minimize that  one of the nice things about this intune  is it seems only to care about ph  so it's impervious to pretty much any  other uh  things that you would add that won't  completely denature it altogether so  again a really nice purification  of a glycoprotein made in mammalian  cells and when we ran that through  a mass spec we found that the peak is a  nice sharp peak on mass spec  and it appears that it's the peak is  within the range that you would expect  for glycosylated epo  and so to wrap up uh this is the  non-protein a platform looking forward  um we believe that we can now put an  entine tag  on just about anything a lot of you are  going to say well i have a protein that  won't express won't fold won't stay  stable and is a total nightmare and i've  tried every tag in existence  this tag may not help you it's really  just to simplify the purification  the rule of thumb is that if you can  purify with a his tag you can probably  purify it with an inseam tag  that's been our experiment experience if  it's something that absolutely won't  behave  then the experience may be different but  but these therapeutic proteins most of  them are made solubly in either  mammalian cells or they're refolded in e  coli  we're working on protocols to re-fold  in teen tag proteins but we haven't run  into any successes or failures  uh yet and we're not that good at  refolding proteins to be perfectly  honest so if you're good at refolding  proteins we would love to work with you  on that  um but the idea is now you can in the  research  you can put a tag on you can purify  things quickly conveniently  high throughput but that same first  purification that you use to purify your  first candidate the first time  can now be scaled completely up into  clinical manufacturing  the same column the same process can be  used to make a drug that you can now  inject into humans because it'll be  fully native  with maybe one or two amino acid changes  at the front end  but it will not have anything resembling  a real tag on it  and so agile low capital investment and  readily platformable  and just to wrap up the protein the  company that we've started and i need to  put back in my my disclaimer since i'm  have ohio state all over this i have to  disclaim and let you know that i'm also  involved with the company  and so ohio state will not back me up on  anything that i've said here  um but but the company that we started  is called protein  proteincapturescience.com i know that  it's a mouthful but  try finding an unused url these days and  this one was actually still free  and we are hoping to launch this product  in the next two to three months um  may june probably at the latest and so  we're looking for people who want to try  it out  and we've budgeted for some free samples  don't ask us for a liter of resin  but if you want a five mil cartridge  like a high trap cartridge that can be  arranged  and we're going to have one mil  cartridges that we can definitely  arrange  and if your protein binds and cleaves  and you become interested in this  technology  then we're hoping that you'll become one  of our one of our first customers  and so if you go to protein capture  science we're still working on the web  page it's a little sketchy i agree  but by the time we launch it's going to  be fully developed but there is a sign  up if you want to go ahead and type in  your email address and write any  questions that you have they come  straight to me  and generally speaking i'm very  responsive and so i just wanted to wrap  up really quick by acknowledging my  group  um the big names on here everybody has  has helped in some way in different  pieces of what i showed you today  but the big one under collaborators and  co-inventors is chiang hwa  he was one of the ones who really  convinced me to look at split in teams  in the first place and he did a lot of  the early development work  and in my lab right now i have joe  terrace jacqueline miozzi  and brian marshall who are developing  the product itself and helping me get it  out the door  um before them michael coolbaugh he's  now at sanofi  uh su chang is at regeneron sam stimple  is  at i believe sanofi also ashwin is at  merck and yaman is at biogen and so  my students have done really really well  finding jobs and honestly  if you're interested in hiring a  top-notch phd or  undergrad you should also my students  are doing extremely well  um and i'm very proud of all of them  so that wraps up my talk and what i  wanted to do is thank you again for  coming  i hope that you're as excited about this  technology as i am i do think it has the  potential to make a significant  impact and i understand that  if you post questions they will be  emailed to me  and i can tell you that i will be very  responsive in answering them  so please email me if you want to set up  a conversation  i'm happy to talk by phone or we can  even set up a zoom meeting  as a professor i'm really really good at  that now  so that's all i'm going to say today and  uh thanks again for coming and i hope  that i will hear from you soon
 

